```ts
// tests/pharmacies.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('pharmacies page shows its heading', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('http://localhost:3000/pharmacies');
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Pharmacies' })).toBeVisible();
});
```

Run it with the browser visible:

```bash
npx playwright test pharmacies.spec.ts --headed
```

lessons.body:
Three objects sit under every Playwright test.

- **Browser**: one launched browser (Chromium, for example). Starting it is slow, so the runner starts it once per worker.
- **Browser context**: a fresh, private browser profile inside that browser, like an incognito window. It has its own cookies, local storage and session. Creating one is fast.
- **Page**: a tab inside a context.

By default each test gets its **own new context and page**. That is the reason tests do not leak state into each other: a todo added in one test is not there in the next. You can see it on the TodoMVC demo, which keeps its list in local storage:

```ts
test('clean context 1 adds', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/');
  // ...adds a todo called "leftover?"
});

test('clean context 2 checks', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/');
  await expect(page.getByTestId('todo-item')).toHaveCount(0);   // passes
});
```

I ran this pair. The second test passes because it gets a brand-new context, not because the first test cleaned up.

**Two users in one test**

Sometimes a test needs two people, such as a dispatcher and a pharmacist. Ask for the `browser` fixture and make two contexts:

```ts
test('two contexts do not share storage', async ({ browser }) => {
  const a = await browser.newContext();
  const b = await browser.newContext();
  const pa = await a.newPage();
  const pb = await b.newPage();
  await pa.goto('https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/');
  await pb.goto('https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/');
  // add a todo in pa only
  await expect(pb.getByTestId('todo-item')).toHaveCount(0);
  await a.close();
  await b.close();
});
```

Two **pages in the same context** share storage and cookies. Two **contexts** do not. I ran that too: a second page in context A saw A's todo; context B saw nothing.

**What isolation does not cover**

A fresh context isolates the browser, not the server. If a test creates a shift in the database, the next test can see it. Server-side data needs its own care, covered in the test data lesson.

**Why this matters**

Isolation lets Playwright run tests in parallel and makes a failure point to one test, not to whatever ran before it.
